---
import ChapterLayout from '../layouts/ChapterLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/content/Section.astro';
import Cite from '../components/content/Cite.astro';
import Note from '../components/content/Note.astro';
import Figure from '../components/content/Figure.astro';
import Table from '../components/content/Table.astro';
import ConceptFlow from '../components/science/ConceptFlow.astro';
import { families } from '../data/signals';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';

const toc = [
  { id: 'the-idea', title: 'The idea in one band' },
  { id: 'what-breath-carries', title: 'What a breath carries' },
  { id: 'three-families', title: 'Three signal families' },
  { id: 'from-electronic-to-biological', title: 'From electronic noses to biological ones' },
  { id: 'how-it-compares', title: 'How the approaches compare' },
  { id: 'what-we-expect', title: 'What we expect, and what we do not claim' },
];

const refs = ['phillips1999', 'machado2005', 'peng2009', 'hakim2012', 'radhika2007', 'nlst2011'];
---

<ChapterLayout
  slug="solution"
  toc={toc}
  refs={refs}
  brief={[
    'OncoNose is an array of engineered biological sensing modules that respond to compounds in exhaled breath.',
    'The modules are deliberately cross-reactive, so the useful information is the pattern across the array rather than any single signal.',
    'Breath sensing with chemical sensor arrays is established in research; doing it with engineered biology is what we are testing.',
  ]}
>
  <Section id="the-idea" title="The idea in one band">
    <p>
      An electronic nose does not identify single molecules the way a mass spectrometer does. It uses an array of
      sensors that each respond to many compounds, and reads the combined pattern.<Cite ids={['machado2005', 'peng2009']} /> OncoNose keeps that
      idea and changes the sensor: instead of chemical films, each element is a living, engineered sensing module.
    </p>
    <Figure
      caption="The concept from breath to screening decision. Each stage is explained step by step in How it works."
      surface="none"
    >
      <ConceptFlow />
    </Figure>
  </Section>

  <Section id="what-breath-carries" title="What a breath carries">
    <Note kind="definition" title="Volatile organic compounds (VOCs)">
      <p>
        Small carbon-based molecules that evaporate easily at body temperature. Hundreds of them are present in exhaled
        breath, at concentrations usually measured in parts per billion.
      </p>
    </Note>
    <p>
      Breath has been studied as a source of cancer markers since the 1990s. An early cross-sectional study reported
      differences in breath VOC profiles between people with and without lung cancer,<Cite id="phillips1999" /> and a
      later review catalogued the compounds reported across studies together with the biochemical pathways that might
      produce them, including oxidative damage to membrane lipids and altered tumour metabolism.<Cite id="hakim2012" />
    </p>
    <Note kind="limitation" title="The literature is not a settled answer">
      <p>
        Published studies differ in which compounds they report, how breath was collected and how many people took part.
        No single breath biomarker is accepted for lung cancer screening today. OncoNose treats these compound classes as
        a starting point for sensor design, not as proven markers.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="three-families" title="Three signal families">
    <p>
      We group candidate compounds into three families, chosen because they correspond to processes described in the
      breath literature.<Cite id="hakim2012" /> The same three colours are used for these families everywhere on this
      site.
    </p>
    <ul class="families">
      {
        families.map((family) => (
          <li data-family={family.id}>
            <span class="families__swatch" aria-hidden="true" />
            <div>
              <h3>{family.name}</h3>
              <p>{family.why}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Because one process can affect many compounds, and one compound can come from many processes, no single module can
      carry the answer. The array is designed so that each module responds mostly to one family and a little to the
      others. That overlap is what turns six numbers into a shape.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="from-electronic-to-biological" title="From electronic noses to biological ones">
    <p>
      Sensor-array approaches have been tested on breath before. In one feasibility study, an electronic nose separated
      the breath of people with lung cancer from controls, reporting 71.4% sensitivity and 91.9% specificity in a
      validation group of 76 people.<Cite id="machado2005" /> Another used gold nanoparticle sensors to distinguish breath
      samples of lung cancer patients from healthy controls.<Cite id="peng2009" /> Both are small research studies, not
      screening programmes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Biology already solves the sensing problem: receptors detect small molecules and cells convert that into a
      response. Those parts can be engineered. A yeast strain has been built carrying the main components of the
      mammalian olfactory signalling pathway, with receptor activation coupled to expression of a fluorescent
      reporter, and used to screen for receptors responding to a specific chemical.<Cite id="radhika2007" />
    </p>
    <Note kind="hypothesis" title="Why this could be worth doing">
      <p>
        Biological sensing elements can be produced by growing cells rather than manufactured, can be swapped without
        redesigning the device, and report through signals such as fluorescence that simple optics can read. Whether
        those advantages survive contact with real breath samples is an open question, and it is the one this project
        exists to test.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>

  <Section id="how-it-compares" title="How the approaches compare">
    <Table label="Comparison of lung cancer screening and breath sensing approaches" note="Evidence level refers to lung cancer specifically.">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Approach</th>
            <th scope="col">What it measures</th>
            <th scope="col">What it needs</th>
            <th scope="col">Evidence for lung cancer</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Low-dose CT</th>
            <td>Anatomy: nodules in the lung</td>
            <td>CT scanner, radiologist, follow-up pathway</td>
            <td>Randomised trials showing reduced mortality<Cite id="nlst2011" /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Electronic nose</th>
            <td>Pattern across chemical sensors</td>
            <td>Sensor array, controlled sampling, trained model</td>
            <td>Small feasibility studies<Cite ids={['machado2005', 'peng2009']} /></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">OncoNose (concept)</th>
            <td>Pattern across engineered biological modules</td>
            <td>Engineered cells, an optical reader, controlled sampling, trained model</td>
            <td>None yet. See <a href={href('results')}>Results</a>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </Table>
  </Section>

  <Section id="what-we-expect" title="What we expect, and what we do not claim">
    <p>We state our expectations as hypotheses so they can be tested and, if wrong, discarded.</p>
    <Note kind="hypothesis" title="H1. Modules respond in a measurable, repeatable way">
      <p>Each module produces a dose-dependent reporter signal to its target compound class at concentrations we can deliver in the lab.</p>
    </Note>
    <Note kind="hypothesis" title="H2. The array separates mixtures that single modules cannot">
      <p>Cross-reactive modules produce patterns that distinguish compound mixtures which look similar to any one module.</p>
    </Note>
    <Note kind="limitation" title="What OncoNose is not">
      <p>
        It is not a diagnostic test, it has not been used on human breath samples, and a pattern is not a disease. Any
        future use would need clinical validation far beyond what an iGEM project can do. The{' '}
        <a href={href('safety-and-security')}>safety and ethics chapter</a> sets out those limits in detail.
      </p>
    </Note>
  </Section>
</ChapterLayout>

<style>
  .families {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--sp-4);
    margin: var(--sp-6) 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .families li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--sp-4);
    align-items: start;
    padding: var(--sp-4);
    border-radius: var(--r-figure);
    background: var(--family-tint);
  }

  .families__swatch {
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--family);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--family-ink);
  }

  .families h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--family-ink);
  }

  .families p {
    margin-top: var(--sp-2);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  @media (min-width: 60rem) {
    .families {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .families li {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
